    "summary": "High‑energy Other One → He's Gone stretch with crisp Truckin'",
    "blurb": "The Lille Fairgrounds show delivers a monster The Other One → He's Gone sequence, a flawless China Cat Sunflower > I Know You Rider, and an inspired Truckin' that rides the band's high‑octane groove. Jerry’s leads are crystal‑clear while the rhythm section stays rock solid throughout. Both soundboard sources capture the performance with excellent fidelity.",
    "review": "May 13, 1972 at Lille Fairgrounds finds the Dead in top form during their European ’72 trek. The first set rolls through a tight early‑summer repertoire—Bertha, Black Throated Wind and a seamless China Cat Sunflower > I Know You Rider that feels effortless. It’s the second set where the night truly ignites: Truckin' launches into a jam that blossoms into an expansive 28‑minute odyssey of The Other One, eventually spilling over into a soaring He's Gone climax that many fans cite as one of the era’s peak moments. Pigpen’s organ adds an eerie undercurrent to the extended improvisations, while Keith’s piano threads melodic depth throughout. The rhythm section—Phil’s steady bass and Bill’s dynamic drumming—keeps everything grounded, allowing Jerry and Bob to weave slashing leads and inventive fills. The show closes with a rousing Not Fade Away > Goin' Down The Road Feeling Bad medley and a sing‑along One More Saturday Night that caps an unforgettable European night.",
